WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:11.660
98. Cats. You're sitting on the couch with your cat on your lap. You're petting your cat,

2
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:18.140
your cat is purring, and you're both very happy. But then you feel your kitty's claws on your leg.

3
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:25.160
She's opening and closing her claws against you. Ouch! Your cat doesn't want to hurt you. She's

4
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:31.620
just kneading. This actually means she's happy and content. Kneading is what cats do to their

5
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:38.220
mothers to make milk flow. When your cat kneads with her paws, she is feeling safe. She's treating

6
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:44.340
you like her mother. Cats also use their paws to show other things. When they are disgusted,

7
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:51.140
for example, they may shake their paws one by one. In fact, cats talk with all their body parts. A

8
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:57.540
loving cat may greet you by rubbing her face against yours. If your cat rears up on her hind

9
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:03.580
legs to greet you, she's trying to get her face close to yours. This means she trusts you and wants

10
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:10.300
to give you a sweet greeting. Cats' tails are important, too. Greeting you with a tail curved

11
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:16.060
like a question mark means she loves you and is happy to see you. Swishing her tail back and forth

12
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:23.060
means your cat is feeling indecisive or aggressive. This is unlike dogs which wag their tails when

13
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:30.180
they're happy. Just like us, cats show emotions with their faces. Flattened ears mean she's scared,

14
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:36.540
as do wide eyes. Blinking slowly, on the other hand, shows that your cat is calm and trusting.

15
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:43.620
Just because we don't use the same language doesn't mean we can't learn to understand animals. Study

16
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.660
cat communication for a few minutes and you'll be amazed at what your cat can tell you.
